B isn't a sentence with a 'context', it's an accepted shortcut for
saying {no lo ro cribe cu nenri le tanxe} (i.e. {lo cribe} exactly
replaces {ma} in {no ma cu nenri le tanxe})

I can't think of any situation/'context' where the blank wouldn't be
equivalent "su'o".

> A negation is hard to get from context alone. I suspect an outer {pa},
> "exactly one of", would be almost as hard.

At most as hard as any other number - which is not that hard. There
are plenty of examples where a certain number would be evident. To the
man with an injured hand you may say:

{ko bevri lo crino li'urta'e}
"do carry the green suitcase(s)"

and be understood to mean "one green suitcase" even if there are 3
green suitcases there. In situations where there is only 1 green
suitcase there, well, let's just say that the number is a lot more
evident.

